{"id": "17cdd9c2-c56f-4872-bdd8-405e0366a643", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T11:25:42.407170", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "5f02e919-cccb-46f7-ac77-5b7a10f924c2", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-bc605f62-5422-47d0-9f53-dd97ec7e7233", "tool_call": {"id": "call-bc605f62-5422-47d0-9f53-dd97ec7e7233", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Initial plan: repo scan + design sketch + assumptions + executability verdict\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## Plan-stage report (no execution, no full design)\\n\\n### 1) Repository & document scan\\n\\n**Present (the entire workspace is a single 482-byte file):**\\n- `/workspace/task.json` — sole artefact. It contains:\\n  - `question`: the verbatim research question about pirate-invasion score-threshold mechanics and hierarchical vs non-hierarchical agent survival duration.\\n  - `context`: \\\"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\\\"\\n  - `documents.track.md`: the literal string `\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"`\\n- Python 3.12.3 is available with the standard library only (`math`, `statistics`, `random`, `concurrent.futures`, `multiprocessing` all import; `numpy`, `scipy`, `pandas`, `statsmodels`, `lifelines`, `matplotlib` are absent).\\n- `multiprocessing.cpu_count()` reports 24 (matches the `cpu_affinity_count`).\\n- `/workspace` is the only read-write mount owned by the task user; `/tmp` is also writable as a tmpfs.\\n\\n**Absent (verified by `find /`, `find /workspace`, and glob for `*.md|*.py|*.csv|*.json|*.txt`):**\\n- No `track.md` file anywhere on the filesystem — it is referenced only as a JSON string in `task.json`.\\n- No game engine, game binary, map file, sprite/asset data, save files, or replay data.\\n- No agent implementation (no hierarchical stack, no flat reactive policy, no shared library/binary).\\n- No mechanics implementation (no score counter, no threshold check, no pirate-invasion event spawner, no hazard model).\\n- No benchmark dataset, no telemetry, no seed log, no prior-run results.\\n- No build artefacts (`Makefile`, `pyproject.toml`, `requirements.txt`, `setup.py`, `Cargo.toml`, etc. — none).\\n- No `pip`-available scientific stack and no network to obtain one.\\n\\n**Immediately relevant to the research question:**\\n- Only `/workspace/task.json`. There is **no source material** that defines the game, defines the agent taxonomies, defines \\\"score threshold\\\", or defines \\\"survival duration\\\". Anything specific will be an *assumption on top of* this task file, not a finding *from* the workspace.\\n\\n### 2) Proposed approach sketch\\n\\n**Harness choice (prerequisite the question forces):** Live gameplay is not possible (no game binary is mounted or reachable). Replayed scenarios are not possible (no replay data exists). Therefore the design must rely on a **controlled, deterministic simulation harness** that *encodes* the relevant mechanics. The harness would be a parameterised Python module that exposes, at minimum, the following interface contract:\\n- `Environment(step, score, threshold, invasions_enabled, rng_seed) -> state`\\n- `AgentHierarchical.decision(state) -> action`\\n- `AgentFlat.decision(state) -> action`\\n- Events: `THRESHOLD_CROSSED → PIRATE_RAID_SPAWN`; `AGENT_HP → 0 → DEATH`; `T = cap → CENSOR`.\\n\\n**Independent variables (full factorial):**\\n- `structure ∈ {hierarchical, flat}` — between-subjects fixed factor.\\n- `trigger ∈ {off, on, on_low_threshold, on_high_threshold}` — propose 3 levels (fully crossed) to recover both the main effect of *whether* the mechanic fires and the dose–response on *when* it fires. Minimum viable is `{off, on}` × 2 structures = 2×2, but the 2×3 design gives an interpretable threshold-sensitivity curve.\\n\\n**Dependent variable:**\\n- `survival_duration`: ticks (or wall-clock-equivalent sim seconds) from episode start until the first `DEATH` event in the agent cohort, or right-censored at `T_cap`.\\n\\n**Control variables (held constant or counter-balanced across cells):**\\n- Per-cell sample size `n` (see assumptions).\\n- Starting resources, starting HP, starting position, starting score = 0.\\n- Resource-generation rate, hazard baseline (background, non-pirate attrition).\\n- Opponent/pirate strength profile (when trigger is on; identical when trigger is off so the off-cell proxies for \\\"no mechanic\\\").\\n- Matched seed sets: each seed index `s` runs in every cell so a structure × trigger contrast is within-seed.\\n- Identical agent count per cohort; identical action primitive set; identical observation window.\\n\\n**Candidate analytical method (primary → secondary):**\\n- Primary: **Kaplan–Meier survival curve per cell + stratified log-rank test** for the main effect of `structure`, of `trigger`, and the **structure × trigger interaction** (joint test). This is the natural fit for a possibly right-censored time-to-event endpoint.\\n- Secondary / model-based: **Cox proportional hazards with structure × trigger interaction**, seed as a random effect (frailty term), to obtain an adjusted hazard ratio with confidence interval.\\n- Sensitivity / classical: **two-way ANOVA on log(survival_duration+1)** as a sanity-check, easiest to communicate even though it ignores censoring.\\n- Effect size: Cohen's d on log-survival for the 2×2 case; HR for the Cox case.\\n\\n**Statistical-wise choices to flag for the design stage:**\\n- Pre-register the censoring rule and `T_cap`.\\n- Pre-register the matched-seed strategy (within-seed pairing vs independent sampling).\\n- Pre-register the test for the interaction (the question's headline contrast).\\n- Holm correction across the three planned log-rank tests to hold family-wise error.\\n\\n### 3) Explicit assumptions and uncertainties\\n\\n**Identity / environment:**\\n- The game title, genre, and platform are not specified in any provided document. The question only references *a generic* \\\"score threshold ⇒ pirate invasion\\\" mechanic. **Assumed:** a generic build-up / tower-defence / colony-survival style environment (e.g., the mechanic pattern exemplified by titles with cumulative score unlocks). *If false, materially changes the design only in cosmetic detail, since events are parameterised.*\\n- Mechanics assumed available: numeric score accumulator, threshold check per tick, scheduled pirate spawn at threshold crossings, agent hit-points, resource generation, action primitives (gather / build / defend / flee). *If a mechanic is absent in the real target game, the design must drop it from the harness and adjust controls.*\\n\\n**Agent taxonomies:**\\n- \\\"Hierarchical agent\\\": a controller with at least two layers — a high-level policy (goal selection / planning) calling a low-level policy (motor / action primitive). **Assumed operationalisation:** explicit stack of two policies with different temporal abstraction.\\n- \\\"Non-hierarchical agent\\\": a flat reactive policy with no internal goal layer — single mapping from observation to action.\\n- *If false (e.g., hierarchy is defined as multi-agent command tree rather than temporal abstraction), the implementation of the two `Agent` classes changes but the factorial design is unaffected.*\\n\\n**Score threshold:**\\n- **Assumed:** a single, fixed, numerical score threshold value per threshold level. The score is computed endogenously by the agent's actions, threshold is exogenous and held by the harness. Two threshold values (`low`, `high`) are explored; `off` means the trigger pathway is bypassed entirely.\\n- *If threshold is a vector, or probabilistic, the design grows from 2 × 3 to 2 × (1 + 2 × k).*\\n\\n**Survival-duration endpoint:**\\n- **Assumed:** time from episode start to first agent death, in sim ticks; right-censored at `T_cap`.\\n- Alternative interpretations to disclose: (a) survival of *all* agents (50% / 100% mortality), (b) mean per-agent survival, (c) time-to-cohort-collapse. These would shift the analysis from a single-event survival curve to a competing-risks or per-agent multilevel survival model.\\n- *If survival is defined differently in the real game, the dependent variable and the right-censoring rule must be changed in lockstep.*\\n\\n**Sample sizing:**\\n- **Assumed baseline:** ≥ 30 episodes per cell (i.e., n ≥ 60 for 2×2, n ≥ 90 for 2×3) to give the log-rank test ~80% power for a moderate effect (HR ≈ 1.5). Seed-matched pairing is the power multiplier.\\n- Heteroskedasticity / non-proportional hazards would require a larger `n`; with the unknown effect size, the 2×3 design needs an a-priori power calculation, ideally driven by a pilot run.\\n- *If `n` cannot reach this, ANOVA-on-logs is a more forgiving fallback but loses censoring information.*\\n\\n**Mechanic isolation (causal-identifiability assumption):**\\n- **Assumed:** the only systematic difference between `trigger = off` and `trigger = on` cells is the pirate invasion force; everything else (map, resources, agent code) is identical. Otherwise the survival delta is confounded with the mechanic.\\n- *If pirate events interact non-trivially with resources or other hazards in the real game, an additional \\\"pirate always-on, non-invasion only\\\" cell would be needed to disentangle.*\\n\\n**Infrastructure / executability (this environment):**\\n- Confirmed in-sandbox: bubblewrap; **network disabled** (DNS and ICMP both fail; `pip install` would also fail); `python3` standard library only; **GPU disabled**; **24 CPU affinity** confirmed by `multiprocessing.cpu_count()` and `nproc`; `concurrent.futures.ProcessPoolExecutor` is available for parallel cell sweeps using only stdlib.\\n- Direct consequence: any executable version of this design **must be stdlib-only** in this sandbox (`random`, `statistics`, `math`, `multiprocessing`, `json`, `csv`). The Kaplan–Meier / log-rank / Cox analyses above cannot be performed with stdlib; they would need either a documented dependency list (numpy, scipy/statsmodels, lifelines, pandas) for an out-of-sandbox run or a from-scratch stdlib reimplementation of the survival routines in the design document.\\n- **Verdict on executability of a *full* experiment here:** not executable as an end-to-end numerical study in this sandbox without (a) stdlib reimplementation of the survival statistics, (b) manually written scoring / threshold / pirate-event mechanics, and (c) hand-coded hierarchical and flat agent policies. The harness design and the analytic-spec design are executable *as documents*; a staged dry run (a few episodes per cell, computing only mean/median/std via stdlib) is feasible as a smoke test, but should not be reported as the answer to the question.\\n- **Recommendation:** A documented harness and dependency list would be required before any analyst could reproduce the experiment. For the design-only deliverable here, the design must therefore state the harness contract, the seed policy, and the analysis pipeline as *specifications* rather than as run artefacts.\\n\\n### Open questions for the researcher (would change the design)\\n1. Is there a specific target game in mind (e.g., a colony / tower / survival title), or is the design meant to be genre-portable?\\n2. How is \\\"hierarchical\\\" defined operationally — temporal-abstraction stacked policies, command-tree multi-agent, or otherwise?\\n3. Is the score threshold a single scalar, a vector (e.g., per-resource), or a distribution?\\n4. Is `survival_duration` first-death, partial-cohort, or full-extinction time?\\n5. Are episodes expected to be censored (time-cap reached), and is parametric (Weibull / log-normal) analysis acceptable as an alternative?\\n6. Is the realistic compute budget for a follow-on run external to this bubblewrap sandbox (so we can spec scipy/lifelines) or in-sandbox stdlib-only?\\n\\nAwaiting the researcher's approval — or any narrowing on the open questions — before drafting the full design (stage=result).\",\"evidence\":\"Repository evidence:\\n- `ls -la /workspace` returns only `task.json` (482 bytes); full text re-verified via `cat /workspace/task.json`.\\n- `find /workspace -type f` returns `/workspace/task.json` as the only file; recursive `find /workspace -name \\\"*.md|*.py|*.csv|*.json|*.txt\\\"` yields no additional artefacts.\\n- `find / -name \\\"track.md\\\"` returns no results anywhere on the filesystem — `track.md` exists only as a string in `task.json`'s `documents` map.\\n- `find / -maxdepth 3 -type f` excluding system mounts returns `/workspace/task.json` as the only non-system file.\\n- `task.json` literally contains `\\\"documents\\\": {\\\"track.md\\\": \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"}`, which is the workspace's own signal that no compute or repository is required.\\n\\nEnvironment evidence:\\n- `python3 --version` → Python 3.12.3 (system Python, no virtualenv on path).\\n- `import numpy|scipy|pandas|lifelines|statsmodels|matplotlib` → all `ModuleNotFoundError`.\\n- `import statistics, math, random, json` → OK; `random.Random` available for seed-specific RNG.\\n- `import concurrent.futures, multiprocessing` → OK; `multiprocessing.cpu_count()` → 24.\\n- `nproc` → 24; `/proc/cpuinfo` model name → Intel(R) Core(TM) Ultra 9 285K.\\n- `ping -c 1 8.8.8.8` → \\\"Operation not permitted\\\" (raw sockets blocked).\\n- `nslookup google.com` → \\\"no servers could be reached\\\" (DNS unresponsive).\\n- `mount` shows `/workspace` is the only read-write mount owned by the task user; `/tmp` is a writable tmpfs; `/usr`, `/bin`, `/lib`, `/lib64`, `/etc/ld.so.cache` are read-only bind mounts from the host.\\n- `/home/actor` exists and is empty; `/tmp` is empty.\\n\\nInference from evidence:\\n- Absence of any game binary, agent code, mechanic implementation, replay data, dataset, or pip-available scientific stack means the experiment can only be specified in this turn, not executed. A stdlib-only staged dry run is the most that could be attempted here, and even that would require authoring the entire harness (environment, agents, score/threshold/invasion mechanics).\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"24 CPU cores will be used via `concurrent.futures.ProcessPoolExecutor` (one cell per worker) for any executable sweep; GPU is not used.</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"Analytical pipeline (Kaplan–Meier + log-rank, Cox with interaction, ANOVA-on-logs sensitivity) is implicitly numpy/scipy/statsmodels/lifelines dependent if executed out of sandbox; stdlib reimplementation is required for any in-sandbox dry run.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Mechanic-isolation assumption: the trigger is the only systematic difference between `trigger=off` and `trigger=on` cells, otherwise the effect is confounded.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Independent observation across seeds, matched-seed pairing across cells; ≥30 episodes/cell target for log-rank power ~80% on HR≈1.5.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Survival duration = time-to-first-death in ticks, right-censored at a cap T_cap. Alternative endpoints (full-extinction, mean per-agent) disclosed in the open questions.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Score threshold is a single scalar per episode; two threshold levels (low, high) plus an \\\"off\\\" cell give a 2×3 design. Threshold is exogenous and held by the harness.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"\\\"Hierarchical agent\\\" = stacked policies with at least two temporal-abstraction layers; \\\"non-hierarchical agent\\\" = flat reactive policy. These are operational placeholders pending researcher clarification.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Game/platform identity and concrete mechanics (score types, pirate behaviour, resources, action primitives) are unspecified in the workspace; I treat them as parameterised placeholders in the harness.</item>\\n\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Whether the follow-on experiment will run inside this bubblewrap sandbox (stdlib-only required) or externally (numpy/scipy/statsmodels/lifelines allowed) — directly determines whether the analysis stage needs a from-scratch stdlib implementation.</item>\",\"$text\":\"Whether pirate invasions interact with other hazards or resources in the real game; would require an additional control cell to keep the mechanic isolated.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Effect-size prior for the interaction: unknown, so sample-size justification relies on a generic HR≈1.5 assumption rather than domain evidence.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether right-censoring is expected to occur often enough to materially affect power, which would shift primary analysis from ANOVA to survival methods.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether \\\"hierarchical\\\" is meant as temporal-abstraction stacked policies, command-tree multi-agent, or another taxonomy — would change the `Agent` class implementation but not the factorial design.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the real target game (if any) defines score as a single scalar, a vector, or a distribution — would change the threshold factor from a 1-D to a k-D treatment.</item>\\n\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-a6cd05ee-b75a-4322-ad95-869616828961", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 36}
