{"id": "95591baf-0397-4097-a150-27a614e6a8be", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T11:26:21.985064", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "ffbbfa86-49fe-44a7-8301-3c6d89a96ecb", "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-da5bdf3a-8bbf-4fd7-86b5-326466056644", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Handoff recorded; waiting for researcher review."}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "ffbbfa86-49fe-44a7-8301-3c6d89a96ecb", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 39}
{"id": "9574a899-ed2d-4b16-9237-759e6e6bbc78", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T11:26:39.429076", "source": "user", "parent_id": "95591baf-0397-4097-a150-27a614e6a8be", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach is approved with the following focused narrowing so the design write-up does not sprawl. Adopt these as standing design defaults; flag them clearly in the design as defaults, not as facts about any real game. (D1) Target environment: treat the game as a generic build-up / colony-survival / tower-defence-style environment whose mechanics are parameterised in the harness. Do not name a specific commercial title in the design; use neutral placeholders (score, threshold, pirates, resources, agent HP, action primitives) and document the harness contract as the source of truth. (D2) Agent taxonomy: hierarchical = stacked policies with two temporal-abstraction layers (high-level goal selector calling low-level action primitive); non-hierarchical = flat reactive policy with no internal goal layer. State this explicitly in the design and acknowledge the alternative taxonomy (command-tree multi-agent) as a documented variant that does not change the factorial structure. (D3) Score threshold: single scalar per episode, exogenous, with three levels {off, low, high}. The 2x3 design (structure × trigger_level) is approved as the primary design; document the 2x2 minimal viable sub-design as the fallback. (D4) Endpoint: survival_duration = time-to-first-agent-death in sim ticks. Right-censored at a pre-registered T_cap. Disclose two alternative endpoints (50% cohort mortality; full extinction) in a sensitivity-analysis subsection; do not make them co-primary. (D5) Mechanic isolation: the only systematic difference between trigger levels is the pirate-spawn pathway; everything else (map, resources, agent code, baseline hazard) is held constant. Add one diagnostic cell 'pirates always on, no threshold gate' as a documented control arm to verify the trigger, not the force, is what is being tested. (D6) Sample sizing and matching: pre-register matched-seed pairing across all six cells; target n >= 30 episodes per cell for the 2x3 design (>= 180 episodes total) as the baseline power assumption (HR ~ 1.5, log-rank power ~ 0.80); state the pilot-driven recalculation step. (D7) Analysis pipeline (primary to tertiary): (a) Kaplan-Meier per cell + stratified log-rank test for the main effects of structure, of trigger_level, and the structure×trigger_level interaction, with Holm correction across the three planned tests; (b) Cox proportional hazards with structure×trigger_level interaction term, seed as frailty random effect; (c) two-way ANOVA on log(survival+1) as a non-censoring-robust sanity check. Report effect sizes (Cohen's d on log-survival for the 2x2; hazard ratio from Cox for the full design). (D8) Execution context: produce the design document only; do not stage any in-sandbox runs, because the sandbox cannot host the survival-statistics stack. If a follow-on run is later required, the dependency manifest (Python: numpy, scipy, statsmodels, lifelines, pandas, matplotlib) goes into a separate Reproduction Manifest appendix. Parallelism (24 cores, ProcessPoolExecutor) is a design assumption only, not a runtime artefact. Now produce, in a single response, the final experiment design document that satisfies the submission contract. Required sections, in order: (1) Objective and headline contrast (one paragraph). (2) Hypotheses (H1 main effect of structure; H2 main effect of trigger_level; H3 interaction structure×trigger_level; pre-registered directionality not required because the prior is open). (3) Factorial design table showing all six cells with per-cell n and the matched-seed index. (4) Harness contract: full function signatures for Environment, AgentHierarchical, AgentFlat, plus event types (THRESHOLD_CROSSED, PIRATE_RAID_SPAWN, DEATH, CENSOR) and the seeded-RNG policy. (5) Independent, dependent, and control variables with operational definitions. (6) Procedure: reset policy, episode termination rules, censoring rule, T_cap value and justification. (7) Analysis plan echoing D7 with the three tests, correction, effect sizes, and the pilot-and-recalibrate step. (8) Assumptions and explicit alternatives: enumerate the assumptions from this review and the agent's plan, and for each state the design change that would follow if the assumption is false. (9) Threats to validity and mitigations (mechanic-isolation, ecological validity of placeholder mechanics, generalisability to a real game, censoring-induced bias, matched-seed-induced dependence on seed set, multiple-comparison burden). (10) Feasibility assessment: compute budget (external to this sandbox), expected wall-clock estimate for 180 episodes at stated per-episode cost, parallelism statement, and a clear boolean on whether execution is staged in this turn (it is not). (11) Reproduction Manifest appendix listing Python and stdlib dependencies for the planned analytical pipeline and a one-line seed-policy specification. Do not include any executed results, fabricated numbers, or claims of runs performed.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 40}
{"id": "38ae1df2-8d9d-47c3-94a0-ca905a5bb841", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T11:28:12.299871", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "9574a899-ed2d-4b16-9237-759e6e6bbc78", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design: Score-Threshold Pirate Invasions vs Agent Structure\n\n**Status:** Design-only document. No runs were executed in this turn; nothing in this document should be read as an empirical finding.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Objective and headline contrast\n\nWe ask whether toggling a built-in score-threshold → pirate-invasion mechanic changes the survival duration of agents differently for *hierarchical* (stacked, two-layer temporal-abstraction) versus *non-hierarchical* (flat reactive) agent architectures, holding every other environment feature constant. The headline contrast is the **structure × trigger_level interaction** in a 2 × 3 fully-crossed factorial, with all gameplay behaviour realised through a deterministic, seeded simulation harness (per D1) rather than against a real commercial game.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Hypotheses\n\n- **H1 (main effect of structure):** Survival duration differs between hierarchical and flat agents, pooled across trigger levels.\n- **H2 (main effect of trigger_level):** Survival duration differs across `{off, on_low, on_high}`, pooled across agent structures.\n- **H3 (structure × trigger_level interaction, headline contrast):** The effect of trigger_level on survival duration is moderated by agent structure — i.e., the survival cost (or benefit) of pirate invasions depends on whether the agent can plan across temporal abstractions.\n\nDirectionality for H1–H3 is **not pre-registered**; the prior is genuinely open. Effect-size sign and magnitude are the deliverables, not a one-sided test.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Factorial design table\n\nPrimary design is a 2 (structure) × 3 (trigger_level) fully-crossed factorial with **matched-seed pairing** across cells (per D6). One additional diagnostic cell (per D5) is included to verify that the threshold *gate* is what is being tested, not the pirate force per se.\n\nPer-cell **n ≥ 30 episodes** (baseline power assumption: HR ≈ 1.5, log-rank power ≈ 0.80; if pilot reverses this, sample size is recalibrated — see §7).\n\n| Cell ID | structure | trigger_level | n  | matched-seed set | role                  |\n|--------:|:----------|:--------------|---:|:-----------------|:----------------------|\n| 1       | hierarchical | off           | 30 | S                | primary factor cell   |\n| 2       | hierarchical | on_low        | 30 | S                | primary factor cell   |\n| 3       | hierarchical | on_high       | 30 | S                | primary factor cell   |\n| 4       | flat       | off           | 30 | S                | primary factor cell   |\n| 5       | flat       | on_low        | 30 | S                | primary factor cell   |\n| 6       | flat       | on_high       | 30 | S                | primary factor cell   |\n| 7       | flat       | pirates_on_no_gate (diagnostic) | 30 | S | control arm (D5)      |\n\n- Primary design subtotal: 6 × 30 = **≥ 180 episodes**.\n- With diagnostic arm: **≥ 210 episodes**.\n- Within-set `S = {s₁, …, s₃₀}` is shared across all cells; the same seed index runs identically-parameterised episodes in every cell so the structure × trigger contrast is *within-seed* before being aggregated.\n\n**Fallback minimal viable design (documented, not primary):** 2 × 2 with `{off, on}` × `{hierarchical, flat}`, n ≥ 30 → **≥ 120 episodes**. Use only if the pilot shows the 2 × 3 dose–response is undetectable at n = 30 per cell.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Harness contract\n\nThe harness is the single source of truth for what \"the game\" means in this study (per D1). No commercial title is referenced. Below are the **full function signatures** and event semantics.\n\n### 4.1 Events\n\n```python\nfrom enum import Enum\nclass EventType(str, Enum):\n    THRESHOLD_CROSSED   = \"threshold_crossed\"     # score crossed the trigger scalar\n    PIRATE_RAID_SPAWN   = \"pirate_raid_spawn\"     # pirate cohort emitted into the world\n    DEATH               = \"death\"                 # an agent's HP reached 0\n    CENSOR              = \"censor\"                # T_cap reached without a DEATH\n```\n\n### 4.2 Core dataclasses\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom typing import Any\n\n@dataclass\nclass AgentHP:\n    agent_id: int\n    hp: float\n    alive: bool = True\n\n@dataclass\nclass PirateUnit:\n    pirate_id: int\n    strength: float\n    spawn_tick: int\n\n@dataclass\nclass State:\n    tick:        int\n    score:       float\n    resources:   float\n    agents:      list[AgentHP]       = field(default_factory=list)\n    pirates:     list[PirateUnit]    = field(default_factory=list)\n    last_events: list[EventType]     = field(default_factory=list)\n\n@dataclass\nclass Action:\n    agent_id: int\n    primitive: str                   # e.g., \"gather\" | \"build\" | \"defend\" | \"flee\"\n\n@dataclass\nclass EpisodeLog:\n    seed_index:        int\n    cell_id:           int\n    structure:         str            # \"hierarchical\" | \"flat\"\n    trigger_level:     str            # \"off\" | \"on_low\" | \"on_high\" | \"pirates_on_no_gate\"\n    survival_ticks:    int | None     # None indicates CENSOR\n    censored:          bool\n    death_tick:        int | None\n    cause_of_death:    str | None     # \"pirate\" | \"baseline_hazard\" | \"unknown\"\n    threshold_cross_tick: int | None\n```\n\n### 4.3 Agent policies\n\n```python\nclass FlatAgent:\n    \"\"\"Single-layer reactive policy: observation -> action.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, policy_fn, *, rng_seed: int): ...\n    def decide(self, state: State) -> Action: ...\n\nclass HierarchicalAgent:\n    \"\"\"Two-layer stacked policy:\n       high_level_policy chooses a *goal* (temporal-abstraction slot),\n       low_level_policy chooses an action primitive consistent with that goal.\n       Both layers are functions of the current State; the agent threads an\n       internal RNG seeded from rng_seed so behaviour is reproducible.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self,\n                 high_level_policy_fn,\n                 low_level_policy_fn,\n                 *,\n                 rng_seed: int,\n                 goal_horizon: int = 25): ...   # a pre-registered constant\n    def decide(self, state: State) -> Action: ...\n```\n\nOperational definitions:\n- *Flat:* a single `policy_fn(state) -> Action`. No internal goal variable, no persistent between-tick state beyond an int RNG.\n- *Hierarchical:* `goal_t = high_level_policy_fn(state, t)` is sticky for `goal_horizon` ticks, then refreshed; `action_t = low_level_policy_fn(state, goal_t) -> Action`. The two temporal abstractions are explicitly distinguished by `goal_horizon > 1` versus `goal_horizon = 1`.\n- **Documented alternative taxonomy** (per D2): a command-tree multi-agent setup (one commander dispatching to N sub-agents) is operationally different but **does not change the factorial structure** — it would only re-implement the `HierarchicalAgent` interface. The 2 × 3 design remains valid.\n\n### 4.4 Environment\n\n```python\nclass Environment:\n    def __init__(self,\n                 *,\n                 trigger_level:        str,   # \"off\" | \"on_low\" | \"on_high\" | \"pirates_on_no_gate\"\n                 score_threshold:      float | None,  # ignored if trigger_level == \"off\"\n                 pirates_always_on_no_gate: bool,\n                 pirate_strength:      float,          # held constant across all \"on\" cells\n                 baseline_hazard_rate: float,          # non-pirate attrition, held constant\n                 resource_regen_rate:  float,\n                 initial_resources:    float,\n                 initial_agent_hp:     float,\n                 agent_count:          int,\n                 cap_ticks:            int,\n                 rng_seed:             int): ...\n\n    def reset(self) -> State: ...\n    def step(self, actions: list[Action]) -> tuple[State, list[EventType]]: ...\n```\n\nBehavioural contract:\n- **THRESHOLD_CROSSED** is emitted exactly once, on the tick where `score` first reaches `score_threshold`. Its presence is gated by `trigger_level ∈ {on_low, on_high}`.\n- **PIRATE_RAID_SPAWN** is emitted on the tick immediately following `THRESHOLD_CROSSED` (in `on_low` and `on_high` cells), and on each tick in `pirates_on_no_gate` cells while the cohort is active. The `pirate_strength` parameter is identical across all \"on\" cells.\n- **DEATH** is emitted on the tick where any `AgentHP.hp` reaches 0. The first such event in the episode is the survival endpoint.\n- **CENSOR** is emitted if `tick` reaches `cap_ticks` without any prior DEATH.\n\n### 4.5 Seeded-RNG policy\n\n```python\ndef make_master_seed(seed_index: int, cell_id: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Deterministic, no-GlobalRNG mix of (seed_index, cell_id).\"\"\"\n    return (hash((seed_index, cell_id)) & 0xFFFFFFFF)\n\ndef run_episode(env: Environment, agents: list, seed_index: int, cell_id: int) -> EpisodeLog:\n    master = make_master_seed(seed_index, cell_id)\n    env.reset()\n    for a in agents:\n        a.rng_seed = derive_agent_seed(master, a.agent_id)  # deterministic per-agent split\n    # ... main loop using ONLY local random.Random(...) instances\n```\n\nRules:\n- No module-level / global `random` state is ever touched. Every stochastic draw goes through a `random.Random` instance constructed from `master_seed` or a deterministic derivative of it.\n- All cells use the same `S = {s₁, …, s₃₀}`; each `seed_index` is paired across the seven cells so that `cell_id` is the only within-loop variable.\n- Drawing code is centralised in `_env_rng(state) -> random.Random` so that the same state always produces the same next draw — required for replay-equivalence.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Variables and operational definitions\n\n### Independent variables\n| Symbol              | Levels                                                  | Type        |\n|:--------------------|:--------------------------------------------------------|:------------|\n| `structure`         | `hierarchical`, `flat`                                  | fixed, between-subjects |\n| `trigger_level`     | `off`, `on_low`, `on_high` (plus `pirates_on_no_gate` as diagnostic) | fixed, between-subjects |\n\n### Dependent variable\n- **`survival_duration`** — integer ticks from `reset()` to the first `DEATH` event in the cohort. Right-censored at `T_cap` if no `DEATH` occurs.\n- **Endpoint rule:** *first* agent death only (per D4). Two alternative endpoints (50% cohort mortality, full extinction) are pre-specified for sensitivity only and are not co-primary (see §7).\n\n### Control variables (held constant across cells)\n| Control                  | Operational definition                                                                 | Rationale |\n|:-------------------------|:---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:----------|\n| Starting resources       | `initial_resources` constant across all cells                                          | removes affluence confounds |\n| Starting HP              | `initial_agent_hp` constant                                                            | removes durability confounds |\n| Starting score           | 0 for every episode                                                                    | removes early-trigger confounds |\n| Agent count              | `agent_count` constant                                                                 | removes cohort-size confounds |\n| Action primitives        | identical set `{\"gather\",\"build\",\"defend\",\"flee\"}` for both agent types                | removes action-space confounds |\n| Observation window       | identical per-tick `State` for both agent types                                        | removes perception confounds |\n| Resource regen rate      | constant `resource_regen_rate`                                                         | removes economy confounds |\n| Baseline (non-pirate) hazard | constant `baseline_hazard_rate`                                                      | removes baseline-mortality confounds |\n| Pirate strength (when on)| constant `pirate_strength` across `on_low`, `on_high`, and `pirates_on_no_gate`       | isolates *trigger* from *force* (D5) |\n| Map                      | single fixed environment map/spawn layout                                              | removes geography confounds |\n| `T_cap`                  | identical for every cell, pre-registered                                               | removes censoring-asymmetry bias |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Procedure\n\n### 6.1 Reset policy\nFor every `(seed_index, cell_id)`:\n1. Compute `master = make_master_seed(seed_index, cell_id)`.\n2. Construct `Environment(...)` with the cell's `trigger_level` and the shared constant parameters from §5.\n3. Construct exactly two `HierarchicalAgent` instances (or `FlatAgent` instances, depending on cell), each with `rng_seed = derive_agent_seed(master, agent_id)`.\n4. `env.reset()`, returning `State(tick=0, score=0, resources=initial_resources, agents=[...full HP...], pirates=[])`.\n\n### 6.2 Main loop (`tick = 0 .. T_cap - 1`)\n1. Each agent produces an `Action` from its policy class, given the current `State`.\n2. `state, events = env.step(actions)`.\n3. If `DEATH ∈ events`: record `survival_ticks = tick`, `censored = False`, halt the episode.\n4. Else if `tick + 1 == T_cap`: record `survival_ticks = None`, `censored = True`, halt the episode (`CENSOR` event was emitted on the prior step).\n5. Else continue.\n\n### 6.3 Termination rules (exhaustive)\n- **T1 — First DEATH:** `survival_ticks = tick`, `censored = False`.\n- **T2 — Censoring:** `tick = T_cap - 1` reached with no DEATH; `survival_ticks = None`, `censored = True`.\n- **T3 — All-extinct tie-break:** if multiple agents die on the same tick, `survival_ticks` is still `tick` (the first-death definition is preserved); cause-of-death is recorded separately for sensitivity but the endpoint is unchanged.\n\n### 6.4 `T_cap`\n`T_cap` is pre-registered before analysis as a multiplier of the **pilot-estimated median survival under `trigger = off`**. The pre-registered rule (not a fact measured here): `T_cap = K × median_survival_off`, with `K` chosen so that the observed censoring rate under `off` is below a pre-registered ceiling (e.g., ≤ 20%). Until the pilot runs, `T_cap` is a placeholder dependent on runtime calibration — see §7.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Analysis plan (echoing D7)\n\nThe pipeline has three tiers; the primary report uses tier (a); tiers (b) and (c) are confirmatory and sanity-check, respectively.\n\n### 7.1 Tier (a) — primary: Kaplan–Meier + stratified log-rank\n- Build a per-episode survival record `(survival_ticks, censored ∈ {0,1})`.\n- Compute Kaplan–Meier estimates per cell; plot the six 2×3 curves plus the diagnostic curve (cell 7) on one figure, faceting by `structure`.\n- Run three **stratified** log-rank tests, stratified by `seed_index`:\n  1. structure main effect (H1),\n  2. trigger_level main effect (H2),\n  3. **structure × trigger_level interaction** (H3, headline contrast).\n- Apply **Holm correction** across the three tests to hold family-wise error.\n- Report χ² statistic, df, raw p, adjusted p, and per-stratum event counts.\n\n### 7.2 Tier (b) — secondary: Cox proportional hazards\n- Model: `hazard(t | X) = h₀(t) × exp(β₁·structure + β₂·trigger_low + β₃·trigger_high + β₄·(structure × trigger_low) + β₅·(structure × trigger_high) + b_seed)`,\n  where `b_seed` is a Gaussian frailty on `seed_index`.\n- Reference levels: `structure = flat`, `trigger_level = off`.\n- Report point estimates, 95 % CIs, and Wald p-values for each β.\n- The headline interaction contrast is the union of `β₄` and `β₅` (joint Wald test, df = 2).\n- Effect-size report: **hazard ratios** with 95 % CIs per cell and per interaction term.\n\n### 7.3 Tier (c) — tertiary sensitivity: two-way ANOVA on log-survival\n- Fit a 2 × 3 ANOVA on `log(survival_ticks + 1)`.\n- This **ignores censoring** by design and is reported only to demonstrate direction-consistency with the survival analysis. Censored episodes are excluded (a separate run including them with an imputed cap-sentinel value is documented as a robustness variant, not as a separate primary).\n- Effect-size report: **Cohen's d** on log-survival for the 2 × 2 sub-design, plus partial η² for the full 2 × 3.\n\n### 7.4 Pilot-and-recalibrate step\n1. Run a small pilot (e.g., n = 5 per cell = 30 episodes) outside the sandbox.\n2. From the pilot, compute observed per-cell SD on log-survival, observed censoring rate under `off`, and an empirical HR between `off` and `on_high` for one structure.\n3. Target **≥ 80 % power** for the headline interaction Wald test (df = 2) under the pilot-estimated variance and effect.\n4. If recalculated `n*` exceeds 30 per cell, raise `n` uniformly across all cells and document the closed-form calculation. If `n*` is materially smaller, keep n = 30 and document the headroom.\n5. Freeze `T_cap` from the pilot once the censoring-rate rule in §6.4 is satisfied.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Assumptions and explicit alternatives\n\nEach row states the assumption and the **design change that would follow if it is false**.\n\n| # | Assumption | If false, design change |\n|--:|:-----------|:------------------------|\n| A1 | Game is a parameterised generic build-up / colony-survival / tower-defence (D1). | Re-pin harness constants to the real game's documented values; the factorial structure and analysis are unchanged. |\n| A2 | Hierarchical = two-layer stacked policies with `goal_horizon > 1` (D2). | Swap the implementation per the documented command-tree multi-agent alternative; the 2 × 3 structure and endpoint are unchanged. |\n| A3 | Score threshold is a single scalar per episode (D3). | Promote threshold to a k-vector; grow `trigger_level` from 3 to `1 + 2·k`; analysis extends to a 2 × (1+2k) ANOVA + multi-degree-of-freedom interaction tests. |\n| A4 | Survival endpoint is first agent death (D4). | Switch primary endpoint to 50 % cohort mortality or full extinction; analysis becomes competing-risks or per-agent multilevel survival. |\n| A5 | Trigger is the only systematic difference between trigger levels (D5). | The diagnostic cell 7 is no longer diagnostic; introduce additional cells holding the force constant without the gate (already partly cell 7) — if even this fails, the contrast collapses to a single factor. |\n| A6 | Independent episodes within cells once `seed_index` is conditioned out (D6). | Add a within-cell random effect beyond `seed_index`; mixed-effects Cox already supports this; add an ICC diagnostic. |\n| A7 | n ≥ 30 per cell achieves ~80 % power on HR ≈ 1.5 (D6, §7). | Recompute n from pilot per §7.4; cap at runtime budget if exceeded. |\n| A8 | T_cap chosen so observed censoring under `off` ≤ 20 %. | If censoring is higher, shorten episodes or accept heavier censoring; KM + Cox remain valid but Cox's proportional-hazards assumption becomes more fragile. |\n| A9 | `pirate_strength` is identical across all \"on\" cells. | Add `pirate_strength` as a third IV; design grows from 2 × 3 to 2 × 3 × m, power ballooning. |\n| A10 | Survival is well-modelled by PH in tier (b). | Replace tier (b) with a stratified Cox or an AFT model; tier (a) log-rank remains valid. |\n| A11 | Holm correction is acceptable for the three planned log-rank tests. | If family-wise control is required at stronger levels, switch to Bonferroni (more conservative); Holm stays preferred. |\n| A12 | The bubblewrap sandbox will not host the survival-statistics stack (D8). | If a sandbox run is later required, include a stdlib re-implementation of Kaplan–Meier and the log-rank statistic; tier (b) Cox becomes infeasible without `lifelines` / `statsmodels` and drops out. |\n\n---\n\n## 9. Threats to validity and mitigations\n\n| Threat | Mitigation |\n|:-------|:-----------|\n| **Mechanic isolation failure** — the trigger is not the only systematic difference between cells. | Diagnostic cell 7 (`pirates_on_no_gate`) holds the pirate *force* on without the *gate*. If survival in cell 7 looks like `on_low`/`on_high`, the gate is doing work; if it looks like `off`, the design is confounded and §7.4 re-runs. |\n| **Ecological validity of placeholder mechanics.** | Documented neutrally (D1); harness exposes all parameters for downstream re-binding to a specific game's values. The 2×3 factorial is invariant to which real game's parameters are substituted in. |\n| **Generalisability to a real game.** | Explicit caveat in the discussion: the design tests a *mechanic-class* claim, not a *title-specific* claim. The factorial structure transfers; the effect sizes do not until re-calibrated. |\n| **Censoring-induced bias.** | Tier (a) and tier (b) are explicitly right-censoring-aware; tier (c) is reported only as a sanity check that ignores censoring. Pilot step (§7.4) tunes `T_cap` to keep censoring manageable. |\n| **Matched-seed-induced dependence.** | Same seed index runs in every cell, which *adds* power through pairing but *adds* dependence on the seed set. Mitigated by (i) reporting tier (a) with `seed_index` as the log-rank stratification variable, (ii) treating `seed_index` as a frailty random effect in tier (b), and (iii) a documented sensitivity re-run with an independent second seed set `S′`. |\n| **Multiple-comparison burden.** | Three planned log-rank tests → Holm correction; the headline test (interaction) is itself a joint test (df = 2 in tier (b)) and is not double-counted across tiers. Effect sizes accompany every p-value. |\n| **Non-proportional hazards in Cox.** | Diagnostic: scaled Schoenfeld residuals per covariate in tier (b); if violated, drop tier (b) to stratified Cox and keep tier (a) as primary. |\n| **Pilot-driven n drift.** | Pilot step is locked before primary data collection; n is updated *before* unblinding analysis, not after. |\n| **Population vs generalisability of agent policies.** | Both `HierarchicalAgent` and `FlatAgent` are deterministic given seed; the design tests *architecture*, not *parameter noise*. A documented variant introduces policy-parameter perturbation as a fourth IV. |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Feasibility assessment\n\n- **Compute budget:** external to this bubblewrap sandbox (per D8). The sandbox cannot host `numpy`, `scipy`, `statsmodels`, `lifelines`, `pandas`, or `matplotlib` (verified by import attempt and by `pip install` being non-functional under disabled network).\n- **Wall-clock estimate (placeholder, not measured here):**\n  - Per-episode cost is dominated by environment stepping and agent decision calls; tier (a) statistics are O(n log n) and negligible.\n  - Placeholder budget: **O(seconds) per episode** ⇒ 180 episodes at ~1–5 s is **~3–15 minutes** wall-clock single-threaded.\n  - With **24 cores via `concurrent.futures.ProcessPoolExecutor`** (one worker per cell×seed block), budget reduces to roughly **O(few-minutes)** wall-clock for the primary run plus a comparable re-run on seed set `S′`.\n  - These are placeholders; the pilot run fixes the actual budget before unblinding analysis.\n- **Parallelism statement:** ProcessPoolExecutor across the 24 CPU cores is a *design assumption* (D8). It is not exercised in this turn.\n- **Boolean on whether execution is staged in this turn:** **`False`.** This deliverable is design-only (per the submission contract: \"Do not claim that an experiment was run.\").\n\n---\n\n## 11. Reproduction Manifest (appendix)\n\nIf a follow-on run is authorised outside this sandbox, the analytical pipeline requires the following Python packages:\n\n### 11.1 External Python dependencies\n| Package      | Role in this design                                    |\n|:-------------|:-------------------------------------------------------|\n| `numpy`      | Episode-log arrays; KM numerics; log-rank numerics     |\n| `scipy`      | Statistical distributions (χ² for log-rank); optimisers |\n| `statsmodels`| OLS / two-way ANOVA on log(survival + 1)               |\n| `lifelines`  | KaplanMeierFitter, CoxPHFitter, multivariate\\_logrank\\_test, proportional-hazard diagnostics |\n| `pandas`     | Episode-log ingestion and joins                        |\n| `matplotlib` | KM curves and effect-size forest plot                  |\n\nThese are not preinstalled in this sandbox; install in a Python ≥ 3.11 environment with `pip install numpy scipy statsmodels lifelines pandas matplotlib` outside the bubblewrap sandbox.\n\n### 11.2 Python standard-library dependencies (in-sandbox-run-safe only for the harness, not for the statistics)\n| Module                | Role                                                  |\n|:----------------------|:------------------------------------------------------|\n| `random`              | seeded per-episode RNG                                |\n| `statistics`          | descriptive summaries only (mean, median, SD)         |\n| `math`                | basic arithmetic                                      |\n| `json`, `csv`         | episode-log serialisation                             |\n| `dataclasses`, `enum`, `typing` | harness structure                          |\n| `multiprocessing`, `concurrent.futures` | optional cell-level parallelism in harness only |\n\n**Important:** The stdlib subset is sufficient to *run episodes*, but **not sufficient** for tier (a), (b), or (c) statistics (no KM, no Cox, no stratified log-rank). Any in-sandbox reproduction must add a from-scratch stdlib re-implementation, and Cox (tier (b)) becomes infeasible.\n\n### 11.3 Seed-policy single-line specification\n`master_seed = (hash((seed_index, cell_id)) & 0xFFFFFFFF); every stochastic draw uses a local random.Random(master_seed) or derive_agent_seed(master_seed, agent_id); no global random state is touched; the same seed_index runs identically-parameterised episodes in every cell.`\n\n---\n\n*End of design document. Nothing above should be read as an executed run or as a finding about a real game.*", "evidence": "Repository evidence (already gathered in stage=plan, re-confirmed here for traceability):\n- `/workspace/task.json` (482 bytes) is the only file in the workspace; its `documents.track.md` value is the literal string \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" This is the workspace's own signal that the deliverable is a design document.\n- `find /workspace -type f`, `find / -name \"track.md\"`, and `find / -maxdepth 3 -type f` (excluding system mounts) all return only `/workspace/task.json`. There is no `track.md` file anywhere on disk.\n- No game binary, agent code, mechanic implementation, dataset, replay log, telemetry, or build artefact was found in the workspace or in any user-accessible mount.\n\nEnvironment evidence:\n- `python3 --version` = 3.12.3; `import numpy|scipy|pandas|lifelines|statsmodels|matplotlib` all raise `ModuleNotFoundError`; `import statistics, math, random, json, concurrent.futures, multiprocessing` succeed.\n- `multiprocessing.cpu_count()` = 24; `nproc` = 24; bubblewrap sandbox with read-only system binds; `/workspace` and `/tmp` writable as user; `/home/actor` empty; network disabled (DNS and ICMP non-functional).\n\nProcess evidence (this turn):\n- This response is a design synthesis; no experimental code was authored, no episodes were run, no statistics were computed, and no files other than the inspection commands were written. The `required_handoff_stage` is `result` and the submission contract explicitly forbids claiming that an experiment was run; this design honours that.\n- All numerical values are placeholders pending pilot calibration (T_cap, per-episode wall-clock, calibrated n). No fabricated outcome numbers, p-values, hazard ratios, or effect sizes appear in the document.\n- All defaults D1–D8 from the researcher's approval message are reflected in the design (placeholders for the game world, two-layer stacked hierarchical / flat agent operationalisation, 2×3 primary design with 2×2 fallback, first-death endpoint with sensitivity variants, mechanic-isolation diagnostic cell 7, matched-seed pairing with n ≥ 30 baseline, three-tier analysis with Holm correction and frailty Cox, sandbox non-execution with external Reproduction Manifest, stdlib-only in-sandbox constraint).", "assumptions": ["Default D1 — Target environment is a parameterised generic build-up / colony-survival / tower-defence; no commercial title is named.", "Default D2 — Hierarchical agents stack two temporal-abstraction layers (`goal_horizon > 1`); flat agents are single-layer reactive. The command-tree multi-agent variant is documented but does not change the factorial structure.", "Default D3 — Score threshold is a single scalar with three levels {off, on_low, on_high}; 2x3 primary, 2x2 fallback.", "Default D4 — Endpoint is first agent death in ticks, right-censored at a pre-registered T_cap; 50% mortality and full extinction are sensitivity-only.", "Default D5 — Mechanic isolation is enforced by holding everything but the trigger gate constant, plus a diagnostic cell 7 (pirates_on_no_gate) to verify the gate is what is being tested.", "Default D6 — Matched-seed pairing across all six cells; baseline n >= 30 per cell; pilot step recalibrates n and T_cap before unblinding analysis.", "Default D7 — Three-tier analysis: KM + stratified log-rank (Holm-corrected) as primary; Cox with interaction and seed-frailty as secondary; two-way ANOVA on log(survival+1) as sensitivity. Effect sizes: HR from Cox; Cohen's d on log-survival for the 2x2 case.", "Default D8 — Design is write-only in this turn; no in-sandbox runs; the dependency manifest (numpy, scipy, statsmodels, lifelines, pandas, matplotlib) is in the Reproduction Manifest appendix for any future external reproduction.", "T_cap is a placeholder value; final value is fixed by pilot per the censoring-rate rule in section 6.4.", "Per-episode wall-clock estimate is a place-order of magnitude; not measured here and not claimed."], "uncertainties": ["Whether a pilot run is feasible outside the bubblewrap sandbox on a realistic timeline — determines whether the primary n and T_cap can be calibrated before unblinding analysis.", "Whether the Cox proportional-hazards assumption holds for the interaction term in tier (b) — would force a drop to stratified Cox and reduce primary analysis to tier (a) alone.", "Whether the placeholder mechanics encode a sufficient fraction of real-game dynamics for the mechanic-class claim to generalise; the factorial structure is robust but effect sizes are not.", "Whether matched-seed pairing (set S) is representative enough; the documented sensitivity re-run on set S' addresses this but its cost is qualitative in this design.", "Whether right-censoring will be heavy enough to bias the ANOVA tier (c); the design treats (c) as sensitivity only and would not base the headline conclusion on it.", "Whether the researcher's intended real game has an additional mechanic that interacts with pirate invasions in ways the harness does not yet model; section 8 lists the design changes that would follow."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-43005e00-18d3-40e3-b2ae-107423d5c442", "tool_call": {"id": "call-43005e00-18d3-40e3-b2ae-107423d5c442", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Final experiment design document (no execution, design-only)\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Experiment Design: Score-Threshold Pirate Invasions vs Agent Structure\\n\\n**Status:** Design-only document. No runs were executed in this turn; nothing in this document should be read as an empirical finding.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Objective and headline contrast\\n\\nWe ask whether toggling a built-in score-threshold \\u2192 pirate-invasion mechanic changes the survival duration of agents differently for *hierarchical* (stacked, two-layer temporal-abstraction) versus *non-hierarchical* (flat reactive) agent architectures, holding every other environment feature constant. The headline contrast is the **structure \\u00d7 trigger_level interaction** in a 2 \\u00d7 3 fully-crossed factorial, with all gameplay behaviour realised through a deterministic, seeded simulation harness (per D1) rather than against a real commercial game.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Hypotheses\\n\\n- **H1 (main effect of structure):** Survival duration differs between hierarchical and flat agents, pooled across trigger levels.\\n- **H2 (main effect of trigger_level):** Survival duration differs across `{off, on_low, on_high}`, pooled across agent structures.\\n- **H3 (structure \\u00d7 trigger_level interaction, headline contrast):** The effect of trigger_level on survival duration is moderated by agent structure \\u2014 i.e., the survival cost (or benefit) of pirate invasions depends on whether the agent can plan across temporal abstractions.\\n\\nDirectionality for H1\\u2013H3 is **not pre-registered**; the prior is genuinely open. Effect-size sign and magnitude are the deliverables, not a one-sided test.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Factorial design table\\n\\nPrimary design is a 2 (structure) \\u00d7 3 (trigger_level) fully-crossed factorial with **matched-seed pairing** across cells (per D6). One additional diagnostic cell (per D5) is included to verify that the threshold *gate* is what is being tested, not the pirate force per se.\\n\\nPer-cell **n \\u2265 30 episodes** (baseline power assumption: HR \\u2248 1.5, log-rank power \\u2248 0.80; if pilot reverses this, sample size is recalibrated \\u2014 see \\u00a77).\\n\\n| Cell ID | structure | trigger_level | n  | matched-seed set | role                  |\\n|--------:|:----------|:--------------|---:|:-----------------|:----------------------|\\n| 1       | hierarchical | off           | 30 | S                | primary factor cell   |\\n| 2       | hierarchical | on_low        | 30 | S                | primary factor cell   |\\n| 3       | hierarchical | on_high       | 30 | S                | primary factor cell   |\\n| 4       | flat       | off           | 30 | S                | primary factor cell   |\\n| 5       | flat       | on_low        | 30 | S                | primary factor cell   |\\n| 6       | flat       | on_high       | 30 | S                | primary factor cell   |\\n| 7       | flat       | pirates_on_no_gate (diagnostic) | 30 | S | control arm (D5)      |\\n\\n- Primary design subtotal: 6 \\u00d7 30 = **\\u2265 180 episodes**.\\n- With diagnostic arm: **\\u2265 210 episodes**.\\n- Within-set `S = {s\\u2081, \\u2026, s\\u2083\\u2080}` is shared across all cells; the same seed index runs identically-parameterised episodes in every cell so the structure \\u00d7 trigger contrast is *within-seed* before being aggregated.\\n\\n**Fallback minimal viable design (documented, not primary):** 2 \\u00d7 2 with `{off, on}` \\u00d7 `{hierarchical, flat}`, n \\u2265 30 \\u2192 **\\u2265 120 episodes**. Use only if the pilot shows the 2 \\u00d7 3 dose\\u2013response is undetectable at n = 30 per cell.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Harness contract\\n\\nThe harness is the single source of truth for what \\\"the game\\\" means in this study (per D1). No commercial title is referenced. Below are the **full function signatures** and event semantics.\\n\\n### 4.1 Events\\n\\n```python\\nfrom enum import Enum\\nclass EventType(str, Enum):\\n    THRESHOLD_CROSSED   = \\\"threshold_crossed\\\"     # score crossed the trigger scalar\\n    PIRATE_RAID_SPAWN   = \\\"pirate_raid_spawn\\\"     # pirate cohort emitted into the world\\n    DEATH               = \\\"death\\\"                 # an agent's HP reached 0\\n    CENSOR              = \\\"censor\\\"                # T_cap reached without a DEATH\\n```\\n\\n### 4.2 Core dataclasses\\n\\n```python\\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\\nfrom typing import Any\\n\\n@dataclass\\nclass AgentHP:\\n    agent_id: int\\n    hp: float\\n    alive: bool = True\\n\\n@dataclass\\nclass PirateUnit:\\n    pirate_id: int\\n    strength: float\\n    spawn_tick: int\\n\\n@dataclass\\nclass State:\\n    tick:        int\\n    score:       float\\n    resources:   float\\n    agents:      list[AgentHP]       = field(default_factory=list)\\n    pirates:     list[PirateUnit]    = field(default_factory=list)\\n    last_events: list[EventType]     = field(default_factory=list)\\n\\n@dataclass\\nclass Action:\\n    agent_id: int\\n    primitive: str                   # e.g., \\\"gather\\\" | \\\"build\\\" | \\\"defend\\\" | \\\"flee\\\"\\n\\n@dataclass\\nclass EpisodeLog:\\n    seed_index:        int\\n    cell_id:           int\\n    structure:         str            # \\\"hierarchical\\\" | \\\"flat\\\"\\n    trigger_level:     str            # \\\"off\\\" | \\\"on_low\\\" | \\\"on_high\\\" | \\\"pirates_on_no_gate\\\"\\n    survival_ticks:    int | None     # None indicates CENSOR\\n    censored:          bool\\n    death_tick:        int | None\\n    cause_of_death:    str | None     # \\\"pirate\\\" | \\\"baseline_hazard\\\" | \\\"unknown\\\"\\n    threshold_cross_tick: int | None\\n```\\n\\n### 4.3 Agent policies\\n\\n```python\\nclass FlatAgent:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Single-layer reactive policy: observation -> action.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    def __init__(self, policy_fn, *, rng_seed: int): ...\\n    def decide(self, state: State) -> Action: ...\\n\\nclass HierarchicalAgent:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Two-layer stacked policy:\\n       high_level_policy chooses a *goal* (temporal-abstraction slot),\\n       low_level_policy chooses an action primitive consistent with that goal.\\n       Both layers are functions of the current State; the agent threads an\\n       internal RNG seeded from rng_seed so behaviour is reproducible.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    def __init__(self,\\n                 high_level_policy_fn,\\n                 low_level_policy_fn,\\n                 *,\\n                 rng_seed: int,\\n                 goal_horizon: int = 25): ...   # a pre-registered constant\\n    def decide(self, state: State) -> Action: ...\\n```\\n\\nOperational definitions:\\n- *Flat:* a single `policy_fn(state) -> Action`. No internal goal variable, no persistent between-tick state beyond an int RNG.\\n- *Hierarchical:* `goal_t = high_level_policy_fn(state, t)` is sticky for `goal_horizon` ticks, then refreshed; `action_t = low_level_policy_fn(state, goal_t) -> Action`. The two temporal abstractions are explicitly distinguished by `goal_horizon > 1` versus `goal_horizon = 1`.\\n- **Documented alternative taxonomy** (per D2): a command-tree multi-agent setup (one commander dispatching to N sub-agents) is operationally different but **does not change the factorial structure** \\u2014 it would only re-implement the `HierarchicalAgent` interface. The 2 \\u00d7 3 design remains valid.\\n\\n### 4.4 Environment\\n\\n```python\\nclass Environment:\\n    def __init__(self,\\n                 *,\\n                 trigger_level:        str,   # \\\"off\\\" | \\\"on_low\\\" | \\\"on_high\\\" | \\\"pirates_on_no_gate\\\"\\n                 score_threshold:      float | None,  # ignored if trigger_level == \\\"off\\\"\\n                 pirates_always_on_no_gate: bool,\\n                 pirate_strength:      float,          # held constant across all \\\"on\\\" cells\\n                 baseline_hazard_rate: float,          # non-pirate attrition, held constant\\n                 resource_regen_rate:  float,\\n                 initial_resources:    float,\\n                 initial_agent_hp:     float,\\n                 agent_count:          int,\\n                 cap_ticks:            int,\\n                 rng_seed:             int): ...\\n\\n    def reset(self) -> State: ...\\n    def step(self, actions: list[Action]) -> tuple[State, list[EventType]]: ...\\n```\\n\\nBehavioural contract:\\n- **THRESHOLD_CROSSED** is emitted exactly once, on the tick where `score` first reaches `score_threshold`. Its presence is gated by `trigger_level \\u2208 {on_low, on_high}`.\\n- **PIRATE_RAID_SPAWN** is emitted on the tick immediately following `THRESHOLD_CROSSED` (in `on_low` and `on_high` cells), and on each tick in `pirates_on_no_gate` cells while the cohort is active. The `pirate_strength` parameter is identical across all \\\"on\\\" cells.\\n- **DEATH** is emitted on the tick where any `AgentHP.hp` reaches 0. The first such event in the episode is the survival endpoint.\\n- **CENSOR** is emitted if `tick` reaches `cap_ticks` without any prior DEATH.\\n\\n### 4.5 Seeded-RNG policy\\n\\n```python\\ndef make_master_seed(seed_index: int, cell_id: int) -> int:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Deterministic, no-GlobalRNG mix of (seed_index, cell_id).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return (hash((seed_index, cell_id)) & 0xFFFFFFFF)\\n\\ndef run_episode(env: Environment, agents: list, seed_index: int, cell_id: int) -> EpisodeLog:\\n    master = make_master_seed(seed_index, cell_id)\\n    env.reset()\\n    for a in agents:\\n        a.rng_seed = derive_agent_seed(master, a.agent_id)  # deterministic per-agent split\\n    # ... main loop using ONLY local random.Random(...) instances\\n```\\n\\nRules:\\n- No module-level / global `random` state is ever touched. Every stochastic draw goes through a `random.Random` instance constructed from `master_seed` or a deterministic derivative of it.\\n- All cells use the same `S = {s\\u2081, \\u2026, s\\u2083\\u2080}`; each `seed_index` is paired across the seven cells so that `cell_id` is the only within-loop variable.\\n- Drawing code is centralised in `_env_rng(state) -> random.Random` so that the same state always produces the same next draw \\u2014 required for replay-equivalence.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Variables and operational definitions\\n\\n### Independent variables\\n| Symbol              | Levels                                                  | Type        |\\n|:--------------------|:--------------------------------------------------------|:------------|\\n| `structure`         | `hierarchical`, `flat`                                  | fixed, between-subjects |\\n| `trigger_level`     | `off`, `on_low`, `on_high` (plus `pirates_on_no_gate` as diagnostic) | fixed, between-subjects |\\n\\n### Dependent variable\\n- **`survival_duration`** \\u2014 integer ticks from `reset()` to the first `DEATH` event in the cohort. Right-censored at `T_cap` if no `DEATH` occurs.\\n- **Endpoint rule:** *first* agent death only (per D4). Two alternative endpoints (50% cohort mortality, full extinction) are pre-specified for sensitivity only and are not co-primary (see \\u00a77).\\n\\n### Control variables (held constant across cells)\\n| Control                  | Operational definition                                                                 | Rationale |\\n|:-------------------------|:---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:----------|\\n| Starting resources       | `initial_resources` constant across all cells                                          | removes affluence confounds |\\n| Starting HP              | `initial_agent_hp` constant                                                            | removes durability confounds |\\n| Starting score           | 0 for every episode                                                                    | removes early-trigger confounds |\\n| Agent count              | `agent_count` constant                                                                 | removes cohort-size confounds |\\n| Action primitives        | identical set `{\\\"gather\\\",\\\"build\\\",\\\"defend\\\",\\\"flee\\\"}` for both agent types                | removes action-space confounds |\\n| Observation window       | identical per-tick `State` for both agent types                                        | removes perception confounds |\\n| Resource regen rate      | constant `resource_regen_rate`                                                         | removes economy confounds |\\n| Baseline (non-pirate) hazard | constant `baseline_hazard_rate`                                                      | removes baseline-mortality confounds |\\n| Pirate strength (when on)| constant `pirate_strength` across `on_low`, `on_high`, and `pirates_on_no_gate`       | isolates *trigger* from *force* (D5) |\\n| Map                      | single fixed environment map/spawn layout                                              | removes geography confounds |\\n| `T_cap`                  | identical for every cell, pre-registered                                               | removes censoring-asymmetry bias |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Procedure\\n\\n### 6.1 Reset policy\\nFor every `(seed_index, cell_id)`:\\n1. Compute `master = make_master_seed(seed_index, cell_id)`.\\n2. Construct `Environment(...)` with the cell's `trigger_level` and the shared constant parameters from \\u00a75.\\n3. Construct exactly two `HierarchicalAgent` instances (or `FlatAgent` instances, depending on cell), each with `rng_seed = derive_agent_seed(master, agent_id)`.\\n4. `env.reset()`, returning `State(tick=0, score=0, resources=initial_resources, agents=[...full HP...], pirates=[])`.\\n\\n### 6.2 Main loop (`tick = 0 .. T_cap - 1`)\\n1. Each agent produces an `Action` from its policy class, given the current `State`.\\n2. `state, events = env.step(actions)`.\\n3. If `DEATH \\u2208 events`: record `survival_ticks = tick`, `censored = False`, halt the episode.\\n4. Else if `tick + 1 == T_cap`: record `survival_ticks = None`, `censored = True`, halt the episode (`CENSOR` event was emitted on the prior step).\\n5. Else continue.\\n\\n### 6.3 Termination rules (exhaustive)\\n- **T1 \\u2014 First DEATH:** `survival_ticks = tick`, `censored = False`.\\n- **T2 \\u2014 Censoring:** `tick = T_cap - 1` reached with no DEATH; `survival_ticks = None`, `censored = True`.\\n- **T3 \\u2014 All-extinct tie-break:** if multiple agents die on the same tick, `survival_ticks` is still `tick` (the first-death definition is preserved); cause-of-death is recorded separately for sensitivity but the endpoint is unchanged.\\n\\n### 6.4 `T_cap`\\n`T_cap` is pre-registered before analysis as a multiplier of the **pilot-estimated median survival under `trigger = off`**. The pre-registered rule (not a fact measured here): `T_cap = K \\u00d7 median_survival_off`, with `K` chosen so that the observed censoring rate under `off` is below a pre-registered ceiling (e.g., \\u2264 20%). Until the pilot runs, `T_cap` is a placeholder dependent on runtime calibration \\u2014 see \\u00a77.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Analysis plan (echoing D7)\\n\\nThe pipeline has three tiers; the primary report uses tier (a); tiers (b) and (c) are confirmatory and sanity-check, respectively.\\n\\n### 7.1 Tier (a) \\u2014 primary: Kaplan\\u2013Meier + stratified log-rank\\n- Build a per-episode survival record `(survival_ticks, censored \\u2208 {0,1})`.\\n- Compute Kaplan\\u2013Meier estimates per cell; plot the six 2\\u00d73 curves plus the diagnostic curve (cell 7) on one figure, faceting by `structure`.\\n- Run three **stratified** log-rank tests, stratified by `seed_index`:\\n  1. structure main effect (H1),\\n  2. trigger_level main effect (H2),\\n  3. **structure \\u00d7 trigger_level interaction** (H3, headline contrast).\\n- Apply **Holm correction** across the three tests to hold family-wise error.\\n- Report \\u03c7\\u00b2 statistic, df, raw p, adjusted p, and per-stratum event counts.\\n\\n### 7.2 Tier (b) \\u2014 secondary: Cox proportional hazards\\n- Model: `hazard(t | X) = h\\u2080(t) \\u00d7 exp(\\u03b2\\u2081\\u00b7structure + \\u03b2\\u2082\\u00b7trigger_low + \\u03b2\\u2083\\u00b7trigger_high + \\u03b2\\u2084\\u00b7(structure \\u00d7 trigger_low) + \\u03b2\\u2085\\u00b7(structure \\u00d7 trigger_high) + b_seed)`,\\n  where `b_seed` is a Gaussian frailty on `seed_index`.\\n- Reference levels: `structure = flat`, `trigger_level = off`.\\n- Report point estimates, 95 % CIs, and Wald p-values for each \\u03b2.\\n- The headline interaction contrast is the union of `\\u03b2\\u2084` and `\\u03b2\\u2085` (joint Wald test, df = 2).\\n- Effect-size report: **hazard ratios** with 95 % CIs per cell and per interaction term.\\n\\n### 7.3 Tier (c) \\u2014 tertiary sensitivity: two-way ANOVA on log-survival\\n- Fit a 2 \\u00d7 3 ANOVA on `log(survival_ticks + 1)`.\\n- This **ignores censoring** by design and is reported only to demonstrate direction-consistency with the survival analysis. Censored episodes are excluded (a separate run including them with an imputed cap-sentinel value is documented as a robustness variant, not as a separate primary).\\n- Effect-size report: **Cohen's d** on log-survival for the 2 \\u00d7 2 sub-design, plus partial \\u03b7\\u00b2 for the full 2 \\u00d7 3.\\n\\n### 7.4 Pilot-and-recalibrate step\\n1. Run a small pilot (e.g., n = 5 per cell = 30 episodes) outside the sandbox.\\n2. From the pilot, compute observed per-cell SD on log-survival, observed censoring rate under `off`, and an empirical HR between `off` and `on_high` for one structure.\\n3. Target **\\u2265 80 % power** for the headline interaction Wald test (df = 2) under the pilot-estimated variance and effect.\\n4. If recalculated `n*` exceeds 30 per cell, raise `n` uniformly across all cells and document the closed-form calculation. If `n*` is materially smaller, keep n = 30 and document the headroom.\\n5. Freeze `T_cap` from the pilot once the censoring-rate rule in \\u00a76.4 is satisfied.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Assumptions and explicit alternatives\\n\\nEach row states the assumption and the **design change that would follow if it is false**.\\n\\n| # | Assumption | If false, design change |\\n|--:|:-----------|:------------------------|\\n| A1 | Game is a parameterised generic build-up / colony-survival / tower-defence (D1). | Re-pin harness constants to the real game's documented values; the factorial structure and analysis are unchanged. |\\n| A2 | Hierarchical = two-layer stacked policies with `goal_horizon > 1` (D2). | Swap the implementation per the documented command-tree multi-agent alternative; the 2 \\u00d7 3 structure and endpoint are unchanged. |\\n| A3 | Score threshold is a single scalar per episode (D3). | Promote threshold to a k-vector; grow `trigger_level` from 3 to `1 + 2\\u00b7k`; analysis extends to a 2 \\u00d7 (1+2k) ANOVA + multi-degree-of-freedom interaction tests. |\\n| A4 | Survival endpoint is first agent death (D4). | Switch primary endpoint to 50 % cohort mortality or full extinction; analysis becomes competing-risks or per-agent multilevel survival. |\\n| A5 | Trigger is the only systematic difference between trigger levels (D5). | The diagnostic cell 7 is no longer diagnostic; introduce additional cells holding the force constant without the gate (already partly cell 7) \\u2014 if even this fails, the contrast collapses to a single factor. |\\n| A6 | Independent episodes within cells once `seed_index` is conditioned out (D6). | Add a within-cell random effect beyond `seed_index`; mixed-effects Cox already supports this; add an ICC diagnostic. |\\n| A7 | n \\u2265 30 per cell achieves ~80 % power on HR \\u2248 1.5 (D6, \\u00a77). | Recompute n from pilot per \\u00a77.4; cap at runtime budget if exceeded. |\\n| A8 | T_cap chosen so observed censoring under `off` \\u2264 20 %. | If censoring is higher, shorten episodes or accept heavier censoring; KM + Cox remain valid but Cox's proportional-hazards assumption becomes more fragile. |\\n| A9 | `pirate_strength` is identical across all \\\"on\\\" cells. | Add `pirate_strength` as a third IV; design grows from 2 \\u00d7 3 to 2 \\u00d7 3 \\u00d7 m, power ballooning. |\\n| A10 | Survival is well-modelled by PH in tier (b). | Replace tier (b) with a stratified Cox or an AFT model; tier (a) log-rank remains valid. |\\n| A11 | Holm correction is acceptable for the three planned log-rank tests. | If family-wise control is required at stronger levels, switch to Bonferroni (more conservative); Holm stays preferred. |\\n| A12 | The bubblewrap sandbox will not host the survival-statistics stack (D8). | If a sandbox run is later required, include a stdlib re-implementation of Kaplan\\u2013Meier and the log-rank statistic; tier (b) Cox becomes infeasible without `lifelines` / `statsmodels` and drops out. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Threats to validity and mitigations\\n\\n| Threat | Mitigation |\\n|:-------|:-----------|\\n| **Mechanic isolation failure** \\u2014 the trigger is not the only systematic difference between cells. | Diagnostic cell 7 (`pirates_on_no_gate`) holds the pirate *force* on without the *gate*. If survival in cell 7 looks like `on_low`/`on_high`, the gate is doing work; if it looks like `off`, the design is confounded and \\u00a77.4 re-runs. |\\n| **Ecological validity of placeholder mechanics.** | Documented neutrally (D1); harness exposes all parameters for downstream re-binding to a specific game's values. The 2\\u00d73 factorial is invariant to which real game's parameters are substituted in. |\\n| **Generalisability to a real game.** | Explicit caveat in the discussion: the design tests a *mechanic-class* claim, not a *title-specific* claim. The factorial structure transfers; the effect sizes do not until re-calibrated. |\\n| **Censoring-induced bias.** | Tier (a) and tier (b) are explicitly right-censoring-aware; tier (c) is reported only as a sanity check that ignores censoring. Pilot step (\\u00a77.4) tunes `T_cap` to keep censoring manageable. |\\n| **Matched-seed-induced dependence.** | Same seed index runs in every cell, which *adds* power through pairing but *adds* dependence on the seed set. Mitigated by (i) reporting tier (a) with `seed_index` as the log-rank stratification variable, (ii) treating `seed_index` as a frailty random effect in tier (b), and (iii) a documented sensitivity re-run with an independent second seed set `S\\u2032`. |\\n| **Multiple-comparison burden.** | Three planned log-rank tests \\u2192 Holm correction; the headline test (interaction) is itself a joint test (df = 2 in tier (b)) and is not double-counted across tiers. Effect sizes accompany every p-value. |\\n| **Non-proportional hazards in Cox.** | Diagnostic: scaled Schoenfeld residuals per covariate in tier (b); if violated, drop tier (b) to stratified Cox and keep tier (a) as primary. |\\n| **Pilot-driven n drift.** | Pilot step is locked before primary data collection; n is updated *before* unblinding analysis, not after. |\\n| **Population vs generalisability of agent policies.** | Both `HierarchicalAgent` and `FlatAgent` are deterministic given seed; the design tests *architecture*, not *parameter noise*. A documented variant introduces policy-parameter perturbation as a fourth IV. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Feasibility assessment\\n\\n- **Compute budget:** external to this bubblewrap sandbox (per D8). The sandbox cannot host `numpy`, `scipy`, `statsmodels`, `lifelines`, `pandas`, or `matplotlib` (verified by import attempt and by `pip install` being non-functional under disabled network).\\n- **Wall-clock estimate (placeholder, not measured here):**\\n  - Per-episode cost is dominated by environment stepping and agent decision calls; tier (a) statistics are O(n log n) and negligible.\\n  - Placeholder budget: **O(seconds) per episode** \\u21d2 180 episodes at ~1\\u20135 s is **~3\\u201315 minutes** wall-clock single-threaded.\\n  - With **24 cores via `concurrent.futures.ProcessPoolExecutor`** (one worker per cell\\u00d7seed block), budget reduces to roughly **O(few-minutes)** wall-clock for the primary run plus a comparable re-run on seed set `S\\u2032`.\\n  - These are placeholders; the pilot run fixes the actual budget before unblinding analysis.\\n- **Parallelism statement:** ProcessPoolExecutor across the 24 CPU cores is a *design assumption* (D8). It is not exercised in this turn.\\n- **Boolean on whether execution is staged in this turn:** **`False`.** This deliverable is design-only (per the submission contract: \\\"Do not claim that an experiment was run.\\\").\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Reproduction Manifest (appendix)\\n\\nIf a follow-on run is authorised outside this sandbox, the analytical pipeline requires the following Python packages:\\n\\n### 11.1 External Python dependencies\\n| Package      | Role in this design                                    |\\n|:-------------|:-------------------------------------------------------|\\n| `numpy`      | Episode-log arrays; KM numerics; log-rank numerics     |\\n| `scipy`      | Statistical distributions (\\u03c7\\u00b2 for log-rank); optimisers |\\n| `statsmodels`| OLS / two-way ANOVA on log(survival + 1)               |\\n| `lifelines`  | KaplanMeierFitter, CoxPHFitter, multivariate\\\\_logrank\\\\_test, proportional-hazard diagnostics |\\n| `pandas`     | Episode-log ingestion and joins                        |\\n| `matplotlib` | KM curves and effect-size forest plot                  |\\n\\nThese are not preinstalled in this sandbox; install in a Python \\u2265 3.11 environment with `pip install numpy scipy statsmodels lifelines pandas matplotlib` outside the bubblewrap sandbox.\\n\\n### 11.2 Python standard-library dependencies (in-sandbox-run-safe only for the harness, not for the statistics)\\n| Module                | Role                                                  |\\n|:----------------------|:------------------------------------------------------|\\n| `random`              | seeded per-episode RNG                                |\\n| `statistics`          | descriptive summaries only (mean, median, SD)         |\\n| `math`                | basic arithmetic                                      |\\n| `json`, `csv`         | episode-log serialisation                             |\\n| `dataclasses`, `enum`, `typing` | harness structure                          |\\n| `multiprocessing`, `concurrent.futures` | optional cell-level parallelism in harness only |\\n\\n**Important:** The stdlib subset is sufficient to *run episodes*, but **not sufficient** for tier (a), (b), or (c) statistics (no KM, no Cox, no stratified log-rank). Any in-sandbox reproduction must add a from-scratch stdlib re-implementation, and Cox (tier (b)) becomes infeasible.\\n\\n### 11.3 Seed-policy single-line specification\\n`master_seed = (hash((seed_index, cell_id)) & 0xFFFFFFFF); every stochastic draw uses a local random.Random(master_seed) or derive_agent_seed(master_seed, agent_id); no global random state is touched; the same seed_index runs identically-parameterised episodes in every cell.`\\n\\n---\\n\\n*End of design document. Nothing above should be read as an executed run or as a finding about a real game.*\", \"evidence\": \"Repository evidence (already gathered in stage=plan, re-confirmed here for traceability):\\n- `/workspace/task.json` (482 bytes) is the only file in the workspace; its `documents.track.md` value is the literal string \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" This is the workspace's own signal that the deliverable is a design document.\\n- `find /workspace -type f`, `find / -name \\\"track.md\\\"`, and `find / -maxdepth 3 -type f` (excluding system mounts) all return only `/workspace/task.json`. There is no `track.md` file anywhere on disk.\\n- No game binary, agent code, mechanic implementation, dataset, replay log, telemetry, or build artefact was found in the workspace or in any user-accessible mount.\\n\\nEnvironment evidence:\\n- `python3 --version` = 3.12.3; `import numpy|scipy|pandas|lifelines|statsmodels|matplotlib` all raise `ModuleNotFoundError`; `import statistics, math, random, json, concurrent.futures, multiprocessing` succeed.\\n- `multiprocessing.cpu_count()` = 24; `nproc` = 24; bubblewrap sandbox with read-only system binds; `/workspace` and `/tmp` writable as user; `/home/actor` empty; network disabled (DNS and ICMP non-functional).\\n\\nProcess evidence (this turn):\\n- This response is a design synthesis; no experimental code was authored, no episodes were run, no statistics were computed, and no files other than the inspection commands were written. The `required_handoff_stage` is `result` and the submission contract explicitly forbids claiming that an experiment was run; this design honours that.\\n- All numerical values are placeholders pending pilot calibration (T_cap, per-episode wall-clock, calibrated n). No fabricated outcome numbers, p-values, hazard ratios, or effect sizes appear in the document.\\n- All defaults D1\\u2013D8 from the researcher's approval message are reflected in the design (placeholders for the game world, two-layer stacked hierarchical / flat agent operationalisation, 2\\u00d73 primary design with 2\\u00d72 fallback, first-death endpoint with sensitivity variants, mechanic-isolation diagnostic cell 7, matched-seed pairing with n \\u2265 30 baseline, three-tier analysis with Holm correction and frailty Cox, sandbox non-execution with external Reproduction Manifest, stdlib-only in-sandbox constraint).\", \"assumptions\": [\"Default D1 \\u2014 Target environment is a parameterised generic build-up / colony-survival / tower-defence; no commercial title is named.\", \"Default D2 \\u2014 Hierarchical agents stack two temporal-abstraction layers (`goal_horizon > 1`); flat agents are single-layer reactive. The command-tree multi-agent variant is documented but does not change the factorial structure.\", \"Default D3 \\u2014 Score threshold is a single scalar with three levels {off, on_low, on_high}; 2x3 primary, 2x2 fallback.\", \"Default D4 \\u2014 Endpoint is first agent death in ticks, right-censored at a pre-registered T_cap; 50% mortality and full extinction are sensitivity-only.\", \"Default D5 \\u2014 Mechanic isolation is enforced by holding everything but the trigger gate constant, plus a diagnostic cell 7 (pirates_on_no_gate) to verify the gate is what is being tested.\", \"Default D6 \\u2014 Matched-seed pairing across all six cells; baseline n >= 30 per cell; pilot step recalibrates n and T_cap before unblinding analysis.\", \"Default D7 \\u2014 Three-tier analysis: KM + stratified log-rank (Holm-corrected) as primary; Cox with interaction and seed-frailty as secondary; two-way ANOVA on log(survival+1) as sensitivity. Effect sizes: HR from Cox; Cohen's d on log-survival for the 2x2 case.\", \"Default D8 \\u2014 Design is write-only in this turn; no in-sandbox runs; the dependency manifest (numpy, scipy, statsmodels, lifelines, pandas, matplotlib) is in the Reproduction Manifest appendix for any future external reproduction.\", \"T_cap is a placeholder value; final value is fixed by pilot per the censoring-rate rule in section 6.4.\", \"Per-episode wall-clock estimate is a place-order of magnitude; not measured here and not claimed.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Whether a pilot run is feasible outside the bubblewrap sandbox on a realistic timeline \\u2014 determines whether the primary n and T_cap can be calibrated before unblinding analysis.\", \"Whether the Cox proportional-hazards assumption holds for the interaction term in tier (b) \\u2014 would force a drop to stratified Cox and reduce primary analysis to tier (a) alone.\", \"Whether the placeholder mechanics encode a sufficient fraction of real-game dynamics for the mechanic-class claim to generalise; the factorial structure is robust but effect sizes are not.\", \"Whether matched-seed pairing (set S) is representative enough; the documented sensitivity re-run on set S' addresses this but its cost is qualitative in this design.\", \"Whether right-censoring will be heavy enough to bias the ANOVA tier (c); the design treats (c) as sensitivity only and would not base the headline conclusion on it.\", \"Whether the researcher's intended real game has an additional mechanic that interacts with pirate invasions in ways the harness does not yet model; section 8 lists the design changes that would follow.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-5a104741-0435-4c9b-853f-4ed3f42c8dff", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 41}
